          "id": "sec-1-1",
          "secNumber": "1.1",
          "heading": "Foundational Principles & Curriculum Scope: Electrostatic Field Analysis, Numerical Field Computation, and Insulation Stress",
          "content": "# Unit 1: Electrostatic fields and insulation stress\n\n## Why this subject matters\n\nA nominal terminal voltage never defines the maximum field in a nonuniform geometry. High-voltage design therefore begins by locating gradients in the electrostatic potential, specifying electrode curvature, and acknowledging the distinction between an ideal metal and a real surface with burrs, coatings, particles, and discontinuities.\n\n## Common reference case and assumptions\n\nA coaxial thought experiment, with ideal radial symmetry, is the common case. Voltage is a boundary condition and the radial field follows from a potential solution; current and power cannot be inferred from this electrostatic result without constitutive properties and a time dependence.\n\n## Outcomes and verification\n\nIdentify boundary conditions, compute field peaks, compare geometries, and reject conclusions unsupported by breakdown statistics or material condition.\n\n## A researcher's reading protocol\n\nBegin by stating the metallic electrodes and dielectric domains. Check continuity of potential, tangential electric field and, where appropriate, normal displacement. Use a mesh-refinement exercise in a numerical solver to determine whether a reported field peak is stable; an unbounded ideal sharp corner should not be assigned a falsely precise stress. The model is intentionally limited to analytical and offline-computation exercises. A student should identify the quantity being predicted and ask what observational evidence would be needed to validate it. The location of the maximum physical field is especially significant: an average stress may conceal a sharp local concentration at a boundary, edge, void, accessory or transient discontinuity.\n\nWhen reading Lessons 1.1\u20131.4, first reconstruct the derivation, then change one variable in the supplied worked example. Carry the physical units consistently and ask whether a plotted curve can be traced back to the same symbol definitions as the equation. Use the [interactive software model](../simulations/model-01.html) after the hand calculation so the plot serves as a diagnostic rather than an opaque answer generator.\n\n## What counts as evidence\n\nThe unit challenge is to separate field geometry from dielectric failure. A stronger computed field is not automatically a breakdown prediction unless the dielectric and time-scale evidence are supplied. Distinguish proof of code execution, numerical agreement with a mathematical model, calibration evidence, dielectric qualification, and safe operation. These are five separate accomplishments; none implies the next automatically. All practice in this book is software-only. Do not build test apparatus, charge capacitors or obtain energized equipment for these exercises.\n\n## Suggested learning route\n\nRead all four lessons and reproduce the arithmetic in a notebook. Complete the project with a baseline case and three documented variations. Attempt the unit assessment before consulting its solution notes. Record a clear status for each conclusion: derived, numerically checked, simulated, empirically supported or unresolved. There are no fabricated laboratory observations in this book.\n"
