          "heading": "Foundational Principles & Curriculum Scope: Conduction and Dielectric Breakdown in Gases: Townsend and Streamer Mechanisms",
          "content": "# Unit 2: Gaseous dielectrics and electrical discharge\n\n## Why this subject matters\n\nAn avalanche grows when electron impact ionization exceeds losses, but a visually smooth calculated voltage curve is not a deterministic ignition guarantee. Gas composition, temperature, pressure, electrode condition, waveform duration, and statistical time lag all affect observed breakdown.\n\n## Common reference case and assumptions\n\nUse the Townsend avalanche and Paschen relations strictly as approximate interpretations of gas discharges in their stated validity regions. A Paschen plot can be instructive and still be wrong for highly nonuniform fields, extremely small separations, streamer-dominated gaps, and changing gas chemistry.\n\n## Outcomes and verification\n\nDistinguish collision processes from streamer evolution, build a dimensionally consistent pressure-gap model, and interpret rather than overgeneralize a breakdown plot.\n\n## A researcher's reading protocol\n\nFor every claimed threshold, document pressure, temperature, gas, waveform and electrode geometry. Put an uncertainty interval around an empirical fit, and describe competing discharge mechanisms. Estimate whether a representative electron transport or avalanche calculation stays within the assumptions of the model. The model is intentionally limited to analytical and offline-computation exercises. A student should identify the quantity being predicted and ask what observational evidence would be needed to validate it. The location of the maximum physical field is especially significant: an average stress may conceal a sharp local concentration at a boundary, edge, void, accessory or transient discontinuity.\n\nWhen reading Lessons 2.1\u20132.4, first reconstruct the derivation, then change one variable in the supplied worked example. Carry the physical units consistently and ask whether a plotted curve can be traced back to the same symbol definitions as the equation. Use the [interactive software model](../simulations/model-02.html) after the hand calculation so the plot serves as a diagnostic rather than an opaque answer generator.\n\n## What counts as evidence\n\nThe unit challenge is recognizing that a fit to one pressure-gap range may be misleading at a new scale. Label extrapolation rather than quietly presenting it as a prediction. Distinguish proof of code execution, numerical agreement with a mathematical model, calibration evidence, dielectric qualification, and safe operation. These are five separate accomplishments; none implies the next automatically. All practice in this book is software-only. Do not build test apparatus, charge capacitors or obtain energized equipment for these exercises.\n\n## Suggested learning route\n\nRead all four lessons and reproduce the arithmetic in a notebook. Complete the project with a baseline case and three documented variations. Attempt the unit assessment before consulting its solution notes. Record a clear status for each conclusion: derived, numerically checked, simulated, empirically supported or unresolved. There are no fabricated laboratory observations in this book.\n"
