          "heading": "Lesson 2.4: Surface flashover, contamination and atmospheric effects",
          "content": "# Lesson 2.4: Surface flashover, contamination and atmospheric effects\n\n**Unit:** Gaseous dielectrics and electrical discharge. **Mode:** theoretical analysis and offline simulation. **Prerequisites:** elementary electromagnetism, circuits, differential equations, logarithms and unit conversion. **Review status:** teaching draft.\n\n## Physical mechanism\n\nA surface path follows a boundary rather than a uniform bulk gas gap; contaminants and water films alter field distribution and leakage current. Pollution severity, wetting, material hydrophobicity and creepage geometry interact. Altitude and humidity influence discharge behavior.\n\n## Governing equations and interpretation\n\nSurface resistance approximation R=\u03c1s L/w is valid only for an ideal uniform sheet with sheet resistivity \u03c1s measured in \u03a9 per square, track length L and effective width w. This is not a flashover-voltage formula.\n\n## Fully stated worked example\n\nFor \u03c1s=100 M\u03a9/square, a simple track of length 60 mm and width 10 mm has R\u2248600 M\u03a9. At 1 kV this model gives 1.67 \u00b5A leakage, with no assertion of flashover threshold.\n\n## Making a controlled decision\n\nSet a baseline, change only one controlled variable, and recalculate the metric using the stated formula. Report the change numerically, including units. Then ask whether the change also introduces an adverse effect: higher stored energy, field enhancement, leakage current, uncertainty, ageing stress, or loss of a valid approximation. End with an evidence request that a qualified technical reviewer could act on.\n\n## Failure modes, model exclusions, and hazard boundary\n\nActual contaminated surfaces have spatially varying moisture, dry bands, heating and arcs; a single sheet resistance cannot replace an insulator test.\n\n## Lesson problems (answer guidance follows)\n\n1. **Reproduce the baseline.** Start with the precise governing relation and numerical values above. Write every symbol, perform the substitutions and express the result in physically appropriate units. Explain the sign, direction or phase reference where relevant.\n2. **Parameter-sensitivity check.** Increase the most sensitive input in the worked example by 10% without silently changing another parameter. Recompute, distinguish relative from absolute change, and identify whether the equation predicts linear, reciprocal, logarithmic, power-law or exponential sensitivity.\n3. **Failure of an assumption.** Describe a physically specific modification to **surface flashover, contamination and atmospheric effects** that renders the chapter's simplified model unreliable. Identify at least two previously missing quantities needed to restore predictive value.\n4. **Evidence and uncertainty.** A student claims the chapter's numerical result proves an insulation component is safe. Draft a technically precise rebuttal: which quantity has actually been calculated, which uncertainty remains, and what category of qualified evidence would be needed?\n5. **Alternative mechanism.** Propose a competing explanation for an observed deviation in the relevant field, current, waveform or diagnostic trend. Give a discriminating independent observation rather than only repeating the original formula.\n\n## Solutions and evaluation guide\n\n**Baseline answer:** For \u03c1s=100 M\u03a9/square, a simple track of length 60 mm and width 10 mm has R\u2248600 M\u03a9. At 1 kV this model gives 1.67 \u00b5A leakage, with no assertion of flashover threshold. Reproduce the numeric substitution and state assumptions before reporting precision.\n\n**Sensitivity answer:** Apply the actual displayed governing equation to one 10% parameter change; do not assume a linear relationship when it contains a logarithm, a quadratic energy term or an exponential.\n\n**Limitation answer:** Actual contaminated surfaces have spatially varying moisture, dry bands, heating and arcs; a single sheet resistance cannot replace an insulator test. These exclusions identify what evidence is still required. A model calculation cannot certify safety or material lifetime.\n\n**Evidence answer:** Record the model result as *analytically calculated*, the simulator as *software output*, and an actual dielectric test as *not performed*. The fifth question requires an independent discriminating observation rather than a restatement of the model.\n\n## Software exercise\n\nUse the [offline unit model](../simulations/model-02.html) to change one variable and interpret the graph using the stated equation. This is not a dielectric-withstand test.\n"
