          "heading": "Foundational Principles & Curriculum Scope: Breakdown Phenomena in Liquid Dielectrics: Commercial Oils and Suspended Particles",
          "content": "# Unit 3: Solid, liquid, and composite insulation\n\n## Why this subject matters\n\nBulk insulation has multiple coupled failure paths. Electrical trees, thermal aging, moisture ingress, void discharge, contamination and interfaces have distinct time scales. A measured permittivity or dielectric-loss tangent cannot by itself predict remaining useful life.\n\n## Common reference case and assumptions\n\nConsider a simplified insulating layer where capacitance and loss depend on geometry and excitation frequency. Make explicit when a parallel dielectric model, a series mixture, or a laminated interface model is being used.\n\n## Outcomes and verification\n\nEstimate stored energy and dielectric losses, explain field redistribution, and contrast intrinsic breakdown with progressive service degradation.\n\n## A researcher's reading protocol\n\nConsider the duty cycle and temperature when comparing materials. Chemical state, morphology, moisture and interfaces are not secondary details, because they can control failure long before a published intrinsic dielectric strength is approached. The model is intentionally limited to analytical and offline-computation exercises. A student should identify the quantity being predicted and ask what observational evidence would be needed to validate it. The location of the maximum physical field is especially significant: an average stress may conceal a sharp local concentration at a boundary, edge, void, accessory or transient discontinuity.\n\nWhen reading Lessons 3.1\u20133.4, first reconstruct the derivation, then change one variable in the supplied worked example. Carry the physical units consistently and ask whether a plotted curve can be traced back to the same symbol definitions as the equation. Use the [interactive software model](../simulations/model-03.html) after the hand calculation so the plot serves as a diagnostic rather than an opaque answer generator.\n\n## What counts as evidence\n\nThe unit challenge is to distinguish a short-duration laboratory number from a decades-long service-life argument without assuming a universal acceleration factor. Distinguish proof of code execution, numerical agreement with a mathematical model, calibration evidence, dielectric qualification, and safe operation. These are five separate accomplishments; none implies the next automatically. All practice in this book is software-only. Do not build test apparatus, charge capacitors or obtain energized equipment for these exercises.\n\n## Suggested learning route\n\nRead all four lessons and reproduce the arithmetic in a notebook. Complete the project with a baseline case and three documented variations. Attempt the unit assessment before consulting its solution notes. Record a clear status for each conclusion: derived, numerically checked, simulated, empirically supported or unresolved. There are no fabricated laboratory observations in this book.\n"
