          "heading": "Foundational Principles & Curriculum Scope: Breakdown in Solid Dielectrics: Intrinsic, Electromechanical, Thermal, and Treeing",
          "content": "# Unit 4: Transient overvoltages and impulse waveforms\n\n## Why this subject matters\n\nLightning and switching events produce fast and slow overvoltages for different physical reasons. The amplitude, front, tail and measurement chain matter; a peak reading alone discards information needed to compare a simulated transient with a test waveform.\n\n## Common reference case and assumptions\n\nUse a normalized double-exponential impulse shape as an illustrative waveform, without pretending its coefficients automatically satisfy IEC-defined wave-shape parameters. A separate time-domain sampling model makes quantization and trigger uncertainty visible.\n\n## Outcomes and verification\n\nDistinguish impulse energy and peak voltage, inspect wave-shape sensitivity, and correctly state what a waveform model has not verified.\n\n## A researcher's reading protocol\n\nDistinguish continuous analytic waveforms from sampled instrument output. State the normalization, time origin, front descriptor, tail descriptor, polarity, uncertainty, and any filtering. Do not silently substitute a double-exponential parameter for a normative impulse parameter. The model is intentionally limited to analytical and offline-computation exercises. A student should identify the quantity being predicted and ask what observational evidence would be needed to validate it. The location of the maximum physical field is especially significant: an average stress may conceal a sharp local concentration at a boundary, edge, void, accessory or transient discontinuity.\n\nWhen reading Lessons 4.1\u20134.4, first reconstruct the derivation, then change one variable in the supplied worked example. Carry the physical units consistently and ask whether a plotted curve can be traced back to the same symbol definitions as the equation. Use the [interactive software model](../simulations/model-04.html) after the hand calculation so the plot serves as a diagnostic rather than an opaque answer generator.\n\n## What counts as evidence\n\nThe unit challenge is avoiding false equivalence between a visually similar synthesized pulse and a verified standard test waveform. Distinguish proof of code execution, numerical agreement with a mathematical model, calibration evidence, dielectric qualification, and safe operation. These are five separate accomplishments; none implies the next automatically. All practice in this book is software-only. Do not build test apparatus, charge capacitors or obtain energized equipment for these exercises.\n\n## Suggested learning route\n\nRead all four lessons and reproduce the arithmetic in a notebook. Complete the project with a baseline case and three documented variations. Attempt the unit assessment before consulting its solution notes. Record a clear status for each conclusion: derived, numerically checked, simulated, empirically supported or unresolved. There are no fabricated laboratory observations in this book.\n"
