          "heading": "Foundational Principles & Curriculum Scope: Generation of High Alternating-Current Voltages: Cascaded and Series-Resonant Transformers",
          "content": "# Unit 6: High-voltage measurement systems and uncertainty\n\n## Why this subject matters\n\nA measurement chain contributes loading, stray coupling, frequency response, calibration uncertainty and data-acquisition errors. Simply dividing a scope trace by a nominal ratio cannot verify a test voltage. Rated voltage alone does not imply impulse bandwidth.\n\n## Common reference case and assumptions\n\nUse low-energy equivalent circuits, uncertainty budgets and synthetic impulse samples to analyze measurement credibility. Physical dividers and energized probes are not assembled. The goal is competence in interpreting certificates and synthetic measurements, not a test-lab construction guide.\n\n## Outcomes and verification\n\nPropagate uncertainty, distinguish random and systematic effects, assess sensor loading and determine when traceable calibration evidence is required.\n\n## A researcher's reading protocol\n\nDistinguish an instrument input from a calibrated complete measurement system. Work through loading, transfer function, resolution, systematic error and uncertainty of the inferred measurand. An instrument display with six decimals need not carry six decimals of credibility. The model is intentionally limited to analytical and offline-computation exercises. A student should identify the quantity being predicted and ask what observational evidence would be needed to validate it. The location of the maximum physical field is especially significant: an average stress may conceal a sharp local concentration at a boundary, edge, void, accessory or transient discontinuity.\n\nWhen reading Lessons 6.1\u20136.4, first reconstruct the derivation, then change one variable in the supplied worked example. Carry the physical units consistently and ask whether a plotted curve can be traced back to the same symbol definitions as the equation. Use the [interactive software model](../simulations/model-06.html) after the hand calculation so the plot serves as a diagnostic rather than an opaque answer generator.\n\n## What counts as evidence\n\nThe unit challenge is reporting what an independently calibrated chain can support, not what its screen is capable of rendering. Distinguish proof of code execution, numerical agreement with a mathematical model, calibration evidence, dielectric qualification, and safe operation. These are five separate accomplishments; none implies the next automatically. All practice in this book is software-only. Do not build test apparatus, charge capacitors or obtain energized equipment for these exercises.\n\n## Suggested learning route\n\nRead all four lessons and reproduce the arithmetic in a notebook. Complete the project with a baseline case and three documented variations. Attempt the unit assessment before consulting its solution notes. Record a clear status for each conclusion: derived, numerically checked, simulated, empirically supported or unresolved. There are no fabricated laboratory observations in this book.\n"
