          "heading": "Foundational Principles & Curriculum Scope: Generation of Impulse Voltages: Single-Stage and Multi-Stage Marx Generators",
          "content": "# Unit 7: Partial discharge and insulation diagnostics\n\n## Why this subject matters\n\nPartial discharge is a localized event that does not completely bridge the electrode separation. The measurable terminal pulse depends on circuit coupling, instrument bandwidth and interference; apparent charge is not necessarily the actual charge transferred inside a void.\n\n## Common reference case and assumptions\n\nAnalyze a synthetic phase-resolved PD display with known injected pulse times. Use charge-based concepts to explain why calibration, noise rejection and a measurement window are necessary before interpreting a pattern.\n\n## Outcomes and verification\n\nInterpret synthetic pulses and their limitations, distinguish diagnostic hypotheses, and design a defensible measurement-evidence report.\n\n## A researcher's reading protocol\n\nDescribe how a calibrated equivalent injection would be interpreted, without building the system. Separate the event detection rule, false-positive prevalence, phase reference and hypothesis about the source of the pulses. Beware of using an attractive phase-resolved image as proof of a unique defect. The model is intentionally limited to analytical and offline-computation exercises. A student should identify the quantity being predicted and ask what observational evidence would be needed to validate it. The location of the maximum physical field is especially significant: an average stress may conceal a sharp local concentration at a boundary, edge, void, accessory or transient discontinuity.\n\nWhen reading Lessons 7.1\u20137.4, first reconstruct the derivation, then change one variable in the supplied worked example. Carry the physical units consistently and ask whether a plotted curve can be traced back to the same symbol definitions as the equation. Use the [interactive software model](../simulations/model-07.html) after the hand calculation so the plot serves as a diagnostic rather than an opaque answer generator.\n\n## What counts as evidence\n\nThe unit challenge is resisting overconfident condition diagnosis from a short synthetic sample. Distinguish proof of code execution, numerical agreement with a mathematical model, calibration evidence, dielectric qualification, and safe operation. These are five separate accomplishments; none implies the next automatically. All practice in this book is software-only. Do not build test apparatus, charge capacitors or obtain energized equipment for these exercises.\n\n## Suggested learning route\n\nRead all four lessons and reproduce the arithmetic in a notebook. Complete the project with a baseline case and three documented variations. Attempt the unit assessment before consulting its solution notes. Record a clear status for each conclusion: derived, numerically checked, simulated, empirically supported or unresolved. There are no fabricated laboratory observations in this book.\n"
