          "heading": "Lesson 7.1: Partial discharge versus complete breakdown",
          "content": "# Lesson 7.1: Partial discharge versus complete breakdown\n\n**Unit:** Partial discharge and insulation diagnostics. **Mode:** theoretical analysis and offline simulation. **Prerequisites:** elementary electromagnetism, circuits, differential equations, logarithms and unit conversion. **Review status:** teaching draft.\n\n## Physical mechanism\n\nLocalized discharge within or along insulation does not necessarily form a full electrode-to-electrode conductive path. Apparent charge is a circuit-dependent measured quantity, not directly the microscopic charge in every void. Inception and extinction may depend on previous cycles.\n\n## Governing equations and interpretation\n\nCharge from a calibrated equivalent injection is q=Cinj \u0394V in a simple capacitive calibration model. State that this is an injected reference charge in the calibration circuit.\n\n## Fully stated worked example\n\nFor Cinj=10 pF and \u0394V=10 V, the mathematical injected charge is 100 pC. This does not establish the size or damage rate of an internal void discharge.\n\n## Reconstructing the model\n\nDefine every boundary condition and symbol before calculating. Derive the supplied relation from a recognizable physical law: Gauss\u2019s law, conservation of charge, electromagnetic-wave continuity, equivalent-circuit impedance or probability bookkeeping as appropriate. If a derivative or spatial gradient appears, write what is being held constant and what geometric symmetry is assumed. The derivation must connect to an experimentally meaningful quantity rather than stop at formal algebra.\n\n## Failure modes, model exclusions, and hazard boundary\n\nInstrument coupling, bandwidth, grounding, PD pulse overlap and electrical noise affect the displayed apparent charge.\n\n## Lesson problems (answer guidance follows)\n\n1. **Reproduce the baseline.** Start with the precise governing relation and numerical values above. Write every symbol, perform the substitutions and express the result in physically appropriate units. Explain the sign, direction or phase reference where relevant.\n2. **Parameter-sensitivity check.** Increase the most sensitive input in the worked example by 10% without silently changing another parameter. Recompute, distinguish relative from absolute change, and identify whether the equation predicts linear, reciprocal, logarithmic, power-law or exponential sensitivity.\n3. **Failure of an assumption.** Describe a physically specific modification to **partial discharge versus complete breakdown** that renders the chapter's simplified model unreliable. Identify at least two previously missing quantities needed to restore predictive value.\n4. **Evidence and uncertainty.** A student claims the chapter's numerical result proves an insulation component is safe. Draft a technically precise rebuttal: which quantity has actually been calculated, which uncertainty remains, and what category of qualified evidence would be needed?\n5. **Alternative mechanism.** Propose a competing explanation for an observed deviation in the relevant field, current, waveform or diagnostic trend. Give a discriminating independent observation rather than only repeating the original formula.\n\n## Solutions and evaluation guide\n\n**Baseline answer:** For Cinj=10 pF and \u0394V=10 V, the mathematical injected charge is 100 pC. This does not establish the size or damage rate of an internal void discharge. Reproduce the numeric substitution and state assumptions before reporting precision.\n\n**Sensitivity answer:** Apply the actual displayed governing equation to one 10% parameter change; do not assume a linear relationship when it contains a logarithm, a quadratic energy term or an exponential.\n\n**Limitation answer:** Instrument coupling, bandwidth, grounding, PD pulse overlap and electrical noise affect the displayed apparent charge. These exclusions identify what evidence is still required. A model calculation cannot certify safety or material lifetime.\n\n**Evidence answer:** Record the model result as *analytically calculated*, the simulator as *software output*, and an actual dielectric test as *not performed*. The fifth question requires an independent discriminating observation rather than a restatement of the model.\n\n## Software exercise\n\nUse the [offline unit model](../simulations/model-07.html) to change one variable and interpret the graph using the stated equation. This is not a dielectric-withstand test.\n"
