          "heading": "Lesson 8.3: Qualification evidence and standard boundaries",
          "content": "# Lesson 8.3: Qualification evidence and standard boundaries\n\n**Unit:** Qualification, condition, and engineering decisions. **Mode:** theoretical analysis and offline simulation. **Prerequisites:** elementary electromagnetism, circuits, differential equations, logarithms and unit conversion. **Review status:** teaching draft.\n\n## Physical mechanism\n\nA design calculation, type test, routine production test, site acceptance record and surveillance result serve different purposes. Formal requirements vary with asset category, jurisdiction and the standard edition incorporated into a contract. A textbook cannot issue certification.\n\n## Governing equations and interpretation\n\nAn evidence matrix maps each claim to a test method, responsible lab, calibration traceability, sample identification, environment and acceptance criterion. A blank field means UNVERIFIED, not assumed compliant.\n\n## Fully stated worked example\n\nFor a proposed withstand claim, write: claim unknown; specimen identification missing; waveform definition requires IEC-standard test plan; measuring-system approval absent; environmental correction absent. The rational status is pending, not pass.\n\n## Comparing alternative mechanisms\n\nWrite down at least two competing explanations for the same observation. One can be the intended low-order model, and the other can be an omitted physical process such as moisture, a floating conductor, spatial inhomogeneity, instrument loading, dielectric ageing, or waveform bandwidth. Explain what additional observation would discriminate the hypotheses. A falsifiable comparison is more useful than repeating a likely answer.\n\n## Failure modes, model exclusions, and hazard boundary\n\nIEC 60060-1:2025 describes test techniques and IEC 60071-1:2019 covers insulation coordination; neither alone is a general worker safety rule.\n\n## Lesson problems (answer guidance follows)\n\n1. **Reproduce the baseline.** Start with the precise governing relation and numerical values above. Write every symbol, perform the substitutions and express the result in physically appropriate units. Explain the sign, direction or phase reference where relevant.\n2. **Parameter-sensitivity check.** Increase the most sensitive input in the worked example by 10% without silently changing another parameter. Recompute, distinguish relative from absolute change, and identify whether the equation predicts linear, reciprocal, logarithmic, power-law or exponential sensitivity.\n3. **Failure of an assumption.** Describe a physically specific modification to **qualification evidence and standard boundaries** that renders the chapter's simplified model unreliable. Identify at least two previously missing quantities needed to restore predictive value.\n4. **Evidence and uncertainty.** A student claims the chapter's numerical result proves an insulation component is safe. Draft a technically precise rebuttal: which quantity has actually been calculated, which uncertainty remains, and what category of qualified evidence would be needed?\n5. **Alternative mechanism.** Propose a competing explanation for an observed deviation in the relevant field, current, waveform or diagnostic trend. Give a discriminating independent observation rather than only repeating the original formula.\n\n## Solutions and evaluation guide\n\n**Baseline answer:** For a proposed withstand claim, write: claim unknown; specimen identification missing; waveform definition requires IEC-standard test plan; measuring-system approval absent; environmental correction absent. The rational status is pending, not pass. Reproduce the numeric substitution and state assumptions before reporting precision.\n\n**Sensitivity answer:** Apply the actual displayed governing equation to one 10% parameter change; do not assume a linear relationship when it contains a logarithm, a quadratic energy term or an exponential.\n\n**Limitation answer:** IEC 60060-1:2025 describes test techniques and IEC 60071-1:2019 covers insulation coordination; neither alone is a general worker safety rule. These exclusions identify what evidence is still required. A model calculation cannot certify safety or material lifetime.\n\n**Evidence answer:** Record the model result as *analytically calculated*, the simulator as *software output*, and an actual dielectric test as *not performed*. The fifth question requires an independent discriminating observation rather than a restatement of the model.\n\n## Software exercise\n\nUse the [offline unit model](../simulations/model-08.html) to change one variable and interpret the graph using the stated equation. This is not a dielectric-withstand test.\n"
